WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.960
Reactome is a free, manually curated knowledge&nbsp;
base that provides a user-friendly platform to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:14.800
access and analyse information on&nbsp;
molecular mechanisms of reactions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:21.680
and pathways. It provides highly detailed pathway&nbsp;
diagrams of molecular processes and descriptions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:28.000
available via the Reactome website and to download&nbsp;
in standard formats such as BioPax and SBML.&nbsp;

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:35.120
The basic unit of Reactome is a reaction, which is&nbsp;
defined as any molecular event; including binding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:40.880
phosphorylation, biochemical catalysis, molecular&nbsp;
transport and spontaneous molecular events.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:46.560
These reactions can involve any biological&nbsp;
molecule, but are typically proteins and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:50.960
small molecules (chemical compounds).
A pathway is defined as a series of connected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:55.760
reactions, with each individual reaction&nbsp;
considered as a single step in the pathway.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:00.400
These are represented as pathways&nbsp;
in detailed molecular diagrams.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:06.240
Examples of pathways include immune system&nbsp;
signalling, the cell cycle and apoptosis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:12.240
In Reactome, we create pathways&nbsp;
using reactions as building blocks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:17.520
Reactions are interconnected based on biological&nbsp;
relevance resulting in the formation of pathways.&nbsp;

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:24.720
The key aspects of Reactome content are quality&nbsp;
and attribution. Pathways are written by expert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:29.040
biologists and independently peer-reviewed&nbsp;
before being included in the database.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:35.280
Reactions always cite scientific literature that&nbsp;
experimentally verifies the described event.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:40.720
These manually curated human pathways&nbsp;
are used as templates to computationally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:45.840
infer equivalent pathways in other species,&nbsp;
including most commonly used model organisms.&nbsp;

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:52.000
High-throughput technologies allow researchers&nbsp;
to identify gene regulation markers and determine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.960
gene expression, protein and metabolite levels&nbsp;
for many thousands of molecules in a single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:02.800
experiment, generating huge amounts of data.&nbsp;
Interpreting these data requires an understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:07.920
of the connections between these molecules.&nbsp;
An effective way to do this is to integrate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:13.920
experimental information with well-annotated&nbsp;
established pathway data. Reactome fulfils this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:20.640
need by providing free access to detailed pathways&nbsp;
combined with analytical tools. Reactome includes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:26.000
tools for analysing the pathway context of your&nbsp;
data, mapping expression or other quantitative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:30.880
data onto pathways or extending pathways&nbsp;
with interactions from external databases.&nbsp;

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:36.720
There are two ways to access the Analysis&nbsp;
submission form. Either clicking the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:42.160
Analyse Data button on the homepage or using the&nbsp;
Analysis button on the Pathway Browser header bar.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:47.280
Upon clicking the Analyze Data, an&nbsp;
interface pops out where you can submit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:52.560
your data and perform various analyses.&nbsp;
The tabs on the left of this interface&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.560 --> 00:03:00.480
indicate the different types of analyses:
• Analyse gene list: pathway enrichment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:01.800
analysis (user input required)
• Analyse gene expression:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:05.200
gene enrichment analysis (user input required)
• Species Comparison: compare human pathways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:08.800
with other species
• Tissue Distribution:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:14.640
compare pathways in various tissue types
You can select the analysis you want to perform,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:24.960
paste in or browse for a file containing&nbsp;
your data, or use an example data set.

